<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="jasmonoyl-amino acid conjugates biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6233" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: Jasmonates Jasmonates| (JA) are an important class of compounds, related to |FRAME:CPD-734|, found throughout the plant kingdom as well as in fungi |CITS:[17513307]|. They participate in a number of biological processes including resistance to herbivores and some pathogens, protection against ozone, senescence, and reproductive development (reviewed in |CITS:[19025383][17513307]|). Many different jasmonate precursors and jasmonate derivatives have biological activity, including |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Amino-Acid-Conjugates &quot;jasmonoyl-amino acid conjugates&quot;|. Different species may have a different array of JA-amino acid conjugates, and the biological activity of each compound may differ between species. For example, |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Isoleucines &quot;jasmonoyl-L-isoleucine&quot;| (JA-Ile) has been shown to have biological activity in a number of different plants, including |FRAME: TAX-3702| |CITS:[15258265][18247047]|, |FRAME:TAX-49451| |CITS:[17273867][17085687]|, |FRAME: TAX-384659| |CITS:[18812165]|, |FRAME: TAX-4513| (barley) |CITS:[9315685]|, |FRAME: TAX-4081| (tomato) |CITS:[18458331]|, |FRAME: TAX-4530| (rice) |CITS:[ 9013895]|, and others. In |FRAME: TAX-3702|, |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Isoleucines &quot;jasmoonyl-L-isoleucine&quot;| promotes the interaction between the COI1 protein (Coronatine-insensitive protein 1) and the TIFY10A (JAZ1) repressor protein. In this manner, JA-Ile is believed to help destabilize the JAZ1 repressor and thereby promote JA-responsive transcription |CITS:[17637677]|. COI1 and JAZ1 homologs have been found in other plants, such as tomato, where they also bind in a JA-Ile-dependent manner |CITS:[18458331]|, indicating that JA-Ile might have a similar function in other plant species. However, this is not a universal phenomenon. For example, a treatment of 100 uM JA-Ile fails to stimulate JA-responsive emissions of volatile sesquiterpenes in |FRAME: TAX-467333|, a weed commonly found in Japan |CITS:[TAMOGAMI07]|. Other differences in biological activity have been observed across species for many other conjugates including |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-ACCs &quot;jasmonoyl-1-aminocyclopropane-1 carboxylate&quot;| (JA-ACC), |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Leucines &quot;jasmonoyl-L-leucine&quot;| (JA-leucine), and |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Valines &quot;jasmoonyl-L-valine&quot;| (JA-valine). Jasmonates are found in nature in two isomeric forms, which can have very different biological effects. For example, the biologically active form of |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Isoleucines &quot;jasmonoyl-L-isoleucine&quot;| is |FRAME: CPD-11259|, while |FRAME: CPD-11232| does not promote JAZ1/COI1 binding &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [19349968]|. Initial studies actually suggested that |FRAME: CPD-11232| was a biologically active phytohormone |CITS: [SEMBDER93][17291501]|, but it is now believed that this was due to impure preparations that contained small amounts of |FRAME: CPD-11259|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; In |FRAME: TAX-49451|, jasmonate signaling is an important part of the response to herbivory |CITS:[17513307]|. The level of JA as well as |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Isoleucines &quot;jasmonoyl-L-isoleucine&quot;|, |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Leucines &quot;jasmonoyl-L-leucine&quot;|, |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Valines &quot;jasmonoyl-L-valine&quot;|, and |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Glutamines &quot;jasmonoyl-L-glutamine&quot;| all rise in response to wounding and treatment with oral secretions from the herbivorous tobacco hornworm |FRAME:TAX-7130|. On the other hand, JA-Trp, JA-Met, and |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-ACCs &quot;jasmonoyl-1-aminocyclopropane-1 carboxylate&quot;| have not been detected in this species |CITS:[17513307]|. |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Isoleucines &quot;jasmonoyl-L-isoleucine&quot;| is involved in herbivore resistance |CITS:[17085687]|, but |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Valines &quot;jasmonoyl-L-valine&quot;| and |FRAME: Jasmonoyl-Leucines &quot;jasmonoyl-L-leucine&quot;| do not appear to stimulate herbivore defense responses |CITS:[18065562]|. It is unclear whether these are storage forms of JA or catabolic intermediates. Another important phytohormone, |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN| (IAA), can be conjugated with several amino acids, as shown in |FRAME:PWY-6219|. Some of these compounds, such as |FRAME: INDOLE-3-ACETYL-ASP|, are targeted for further breakdown (|CITS:[9733548]| while others, such as, |FRAME: INDOLE-3-ACETYL-ALA|, may serve as storage forms. A family of amidohydrolases can release free, active IAA from some of these &quot;storage&quot; IAA conjugates |CITS:[7792259][10072397]|. To date, no corresponding JA-amidohydrolases were identified in plants, although an enzyme that can catalyze this activity has been detected in the fungus |FRAME: TAX-45133| |CITS:[9141491]|. Further work will be required to determine the metabolic and functional fate of the &quot;inactive&quot; JA-amino acid conjugates identified in |FRAME: TAX-49451|. Two enzymes that catalyze the conjugation of JA with amino acids, encoded by |FRAME: G-11278| and |FRAME: G-11279|, have been characterized from |FRAME: TAX-49451|. Reduced levels of their transcripts lead to lower levels of JA-Ile, JA-Leu, and JA-Val, suggesting that the &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; synthesis of these compounds requires these enzymes. JA-Gln levels are not affected in these mutants, indicating that at least one other enzyme capable of generating jasmonyl-amino acid conjugates exists in this species |CITS:[17273867][17085687]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="dreher" />
  </Authors>
